import{_ as e,o as t,c as a,Q as o}from"./chunks/framework.b67c47e6.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Multipanel Groups","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/management/multipanel_groups/index.md","filePath":"guide/management/multipanel_groups/index.md","lastUpdated":1790346505000}'),r={name:"guide/management/multipanel_groups/index.md"},n=o('<h1 id="multipanel-groups" tabindex="-1">Multipanel Groups <a class="header-anchor" href="#multipanel-groups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Multipanel Groups&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Multipanel Groups let you combine multiple wall panels into a single unit that is processed together through the machine. This is a production-level decision — you create groups inside a batch to tell the machine to feed several panels consecutively as one run.</p><p>When a group is <strong>valid</strong>, the report system treats the whole group as a single logical panel: Bill of Materials reports use the combined rail length, and machine files (MOB2) are generated as one file for the whole group.</p><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Feature flag</p><p>Multipanel Groups must be enabled on your company by a Produuz.it administrator. Once enabled, a <strong>Multipanel Groups</strong> entry appears in each project&#39;s menu, and admins get a <strong>Multipanel Configurations</strong> table in <strong>Projects Settings</strong>.</p></div><nav class="table-of-contents"><ul><li><a href="#configuration">Configuration</a></li><li><a href="#creating-a-group">Creating a Group</a></li><li><a href="#validation-requirements">Validation Requirements</a></li><li><a href="#group-status">Group Status</a></li><li><a href="#report-form-—-assembly-selector">Report Form — Assembly Selector</a></li><li><a href="#bill-of-materials-impact">Bill of Materials Impact</a></li><li><a href="#machine-file-impact-mob2">Machine File Impact (MOB2)</a></li><li><a href="#tracking-production-progress">Tracking Production Progress</a></li><li><a href="#clearing-proofs-in-bulk">Clearing Proofs in Bulk</a></li></ul></nav><h2 id="configuration" tabindex="-1">Configuration <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Configuration&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The constraints for groups are kept in <strong>multipanel configurations</strong>, shared by the whole company. An admin manages them in <strong>Projects Settings → Multipanel Configurations</strong>: click <strong>New</strong> to add one, or the pencil to edit it. A company can have several, for example one per machine.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Name</strong></td><td>Name of the configuration.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Default for new projects</strong></td><td>New projects use this configuration.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min. height (mm)</strong></td><td>Minimum panel height allowed in a group.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min. / Max. element width (mm)</strong></td><td>Allowed width range for individual panels.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Max. transport width (mm)</strong></td><td>The combined width of all panels plus gaps must not exceed this value. This is typically the physical limit of the machine or transport system.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Min. / Max. elements per group</strong></td><td>Minimum and maximum number of panels per group.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Default gap (mm)</strong></td><td>Default gap between elements when creating a new group.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each project picks the configuration it uses in <strong>Project Settings → Multipanel Config</strong>.</p><h2 id="creating-a-group" tabindex="-1">Creating a Group <a class="header-anchor" href="#creating-a-group" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Creating a Group&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open <strong>Multipanel Groups</strong> in the project menu, choose a <strong>Batch</strong>, and click the <strong>Multipanel</strong> button in the table toolbar. The dialog has two sides:</p><ol><li><strong>Select panels</strong> (left) — tick two or more assemblies of the batch. Once a panel is selected, incompatible panels (different height or rail profile) are disabled; hover one to see why.</li><li><strong>Gaps between panels</strong> (right) — set the gap between each pair of adjacent panels, in mm.</li><li><strong>Validation</strong> — the checks run on their own every time the selection or a gap changes. Each requirement shows a pass or fail icon with a detail message.</li><li><strong>Group name</strong> — give the group a name and click <strong>Create</strong>.</li></ol><p><strong>Create</strong> is only enabled when every check passes, so a new group is always saved as <strong>valid</strong>. Use the pencil on a row to edit a group, and <strong>Update</strong> to save it.</p><h2 id="validation-requirements" tabindex="-1">Validation Requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#validation-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Validation Requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All 7 requirements must pass before a group can be created.</p><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Requirement</th><th>What is checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Height equality</strong></td><td>All panels must have the same height; each must meet the configured minimum.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Element width &amp; transport width</strong></td><td>Each panel&#39;s width must be within the configured [min, max] range, and the total width must not exceed the maximum transport width.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Element count</strong></td><td>The number of panels must be within the configured minimum and maximum.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Top/bottom rail framing</strong></td><td>Top and bottom rail profiles must be identical across all panels.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Same batch</strong></td><td>All panels must belong to the same batch.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Identical zones</strong></td><td>The zones checked by the configuration must be present in all panels or in none, with the same branches in each.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Gap &amp; total transport width</strong></td><td>Total combined width (sum of panel widths + all gaps) must not exceed the configured maximum.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Saw and milling operations on the panel layer must also be equal across all panels. That is checked when the machine file is generated, not when the group is created.</p><h2 id="group-status" tabindex="-1">Group Status <a class="header-anchor" href="#group-status" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Group Status&quot;">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>draft</code></td><td>Not validated yet.</td></tr><tr><td><code>valid</code></td><td>All validation checks passed. Reports and machine files treat the group as a single panel.</td></tr><tr><td><code>invalid</code></td><td>One or more validation checks failed.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <strong>Valid</strong> column of the table shows the result of the last validation; hover it for the details of each check. Click the refresh icon on a row to run the validation again, for example after a design change.</p><h2 id="report-form-—-assembly-selector" tabindex="-1">Report Form — Assembly Selector <a class="header-anchor" href="#report-form-—-assembly-selector" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Report Form — Assembly Selector&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When building a report (BoM, machine files, etc.), the assembly selector is aware of multipanel groups:</p><ul><li>Each assembly that belongs to a group shows a <strong>chip</strong> with the group name and the panel count (e.g. <code>Group A ×3</code>). This makes it easy to spot which assemblies are part of a combined unit.</li><li>A <strong>Force multipanel selection</strong> switch, on by default, sits above the selector. When it is on, selecting any assembly in a group automatically selects all the other assemblies of the same group. This prevents accidentally generating a report for only part of a group.</li></ul><p>A group is only treated as one panel when <strong>all</strong> of its panels are in the selection. If only some are selected, each of them is handled as an individual panel.</p><h2 id="bill-of-materials-impact" tabindex="-1">Bill of Materials Impact <a class="header-anchor" href="#bill-of-materials-impact" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bill of Materials Impact&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When generating a BoM report (Wood BoM, Plates BoM, etc.) that includes all the assemblies of a <strong>valid</strong> Multipanel Group:</p><ul><li>The top rail and bottom rail parts are replaced by a single virtual part spanning the full group width.</li><li>The rail <strong>length</strong> in the cut list equals <code>sum of all panel widths + sum of all gaps between panels</code>.</li><li>All other part properties (material, profile, finish, etc.) are taken from the first panel&#39;s rail.</li><li>Non-rail parts from each panel appear individually in the list, as usual.</li><li>Only one row appears per rail category (one top rail, one bottom rail) for the entire group — not one per panel.</li></ul><p>This ensures the cut list reflects the actual piece that goes through the machine, rather than individual per-panel lengths that would be incorrect.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Example</p><p>Two panels: 1200 mm and 900 mm wide, with a 10 mm gap between them. Combined rail length = 1200 + 900 + 10 = <strong>2110 mm</strong>. The BoM shows one top rail and one bottom rail, each 2110 mm long.</p></div><h2 id="machine-file-impact-mob2" tabindex="-1">Machine File Impact (MOB2) <a class="header-anchor" href="#machine-file-impact-mob2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Machine File Impact (MOB2)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When generating a MOB2 machine file for a selection that includes all the assemblies of a <strong>valid</strong> Multipanel Group:</p><ul><li>A <strong>single <code>.mob2</code> file</strong> is generated for the entire group, named after the group.</li><li>The file is placed in the batch folder as <code>&lt;phase&gt;/&lt;batch&gt;/&lt;group_name&gt;.mob2</code>.</li><li>Part positions within the combined file are offset correctly: parts from panel 2 are placed after panel 1, with the configured gap applied — so the machine sees the full combined run in the correct order.</li><li>The revision field is left blank for group files (since a group has no single design revision).</li></ul><h2 id="tracking-production-progress" tabindex="-1">Tracking Production Progress <a class="header-anchor" href="#tracking-production-progress" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tracking Production Progress&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both the left-menu list (in the 3D viewer) and the <strong>Multipanel Groups</strong> management table show a row of small <strong>station chips</strong> for each group. Each chip represents one station of the batch&#39;s production line and shows how many panels of the group are ready at that station, in the form <code>ready/total</code>:</p><ul><li>An <strong>outlined</strong> chip means no panel has started the station yet.</li><li>A <strong>filled orange</strong> chip means some panels are working there or are already ready there.</li><li>A <strong>filled green</strong> chip means every panel of the group has been marked ready at that station.</li><li>When every station of every panel is ready, a single <strong>Ready</strong> chip replaces the row.</li></ul><p>Stations that none of the panels pass through are left out. Hover any chip to see the full breakdown — number of panels that are ready, currently working, and still waiting at that station.</p><p>In the viewer, open the group row to work on all its panels at once with the <a href="/guide/viewer/#multiwall-checklist">Multiwall Checklist</a>.</p><h2 id="clearing-proofs-in-bulk" tabindex="-1">Clearing Proofs in Bulk <a class="header-anchor" href="#clearing-proofs-in-bulk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Clearing Proofs in Bulk&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the <strong>Multipanel Groups</strong> management table, ticking the checkbox of one or more rows reveals a <strong>Proof Options</strong> menu in the toolbar. The <strong>Clear proof</strong> action wipes all production proofs for every panel of the selected groups in one operation — useful when a group needs to be reworked from the start. The same menu also offers <strong>Set Ready</strong> and <strong>Merge checklist</strong>, mirroring the actions available on individual constructibles. Next to it, <strong>Launch action</strong> generates the drawings of the selected groups.</p>',39),i=[n];function s(l,h,d,p,c,g){return t(),a("div",null,i)}const f=e(r,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,f as default};
